  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nTemperatures across western Minnesota are well on their way to \nmaking a run at 90 this afternoon with winds gusting to around 40 \nMPH. The stronger winds are still working their way to the east \nwhere gusts are only around 25-30 MPH. Fortunately, Tds have \nremained in the 50s so RH values have not fallen below 25%. An SPS \nfor elevated fire weather concerns still seems sufficient, so no \nplans to upgrade to a Red Flag Warning. This heat will continue \nthrough Sunday and likely into Monday now as the timing of the FROPA \nhas slowed down considerably. Record highs are almost certainly in \njeopardy tomorrow, but will likely be out of reach for Monday. \nRegarding the rain chances for Monday, northern Minnesota down into \ncentral Minnesota and northern Wisconsin have the highest chance of \nseeing some afternoon showers, but QPF amounts remain low. \n\nBeginning Tuesday, it will feel much more October-like with highs \nreturning to the 60s. Precip chances increase mid to late in the \nweek, but there is still a fair amount of uncertainty regarding what \nhappens with a cut off low over the Rockies. PoPs came in slightly \nlower with this forecast cycle, likely due to the deterministic Euro \ncontinuing to hold on to the cut off low over the Four Corners \nregion. Other solutions are more progressive and have a shortwave \ndigging across the International Border late Thursday. So, we are \nstill carrying the same amount of uncertainty mentioned in the \nprevious discussion. Hopefully guidance comes into better agreement \nin the coming days. Beyond this, temperatures look to fall into the \n50s with next Saturday morning starting out in the 30s. However, \nlooking into late next weekend, ensembles hint that we are probably \nnot done with temperatures in the upper 60s just yet.",
